WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.350
The geological timescale began as a way to correlate rocks using

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:10.070
fossil succession. Major changes in assemblages marked boundaries before precise numerical

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:16.010
ages were available. Paleozoic, Mesozoic and Cenozoic are eras, each divided

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:20.330
into periods. Precambrian is a broad span containing its own subdivisions,

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:22.370
rather than an equivalent fourth era.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:27.750
From oldest to youngest, the Paleozoic periods are Cambrian, Ordovician, Silurian,

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:30.590
Devonian, Mississippian, Pennsylvanian and Permian.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:35.310
Then come Triassic, Jurassic and Cretaceous in the Mesozoic, followed by

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.610
Paleogene, Neogene and Quaternary in the Cenozoic.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:42.090
The course uses these North American Paleozoic subdivisions;

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.110
some other charts combine two as the Carboniferous.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:49.240
An interbedded volcanic layer can be dated isotopically.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:53.240
A fossil-bearing bed above an older volcanic unit and below a

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.420
younger one lies between those eruption ages.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:59.140
The fossils then help transfer that age constraint to other sections.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:02.380
Watch the two limits close around the bed without pretending they

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.240
provide a single exact date.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.620
A dike is younger than the fossil bed it cuts.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:11.020
If clasts of that dike occur in a later bed, the

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:12.920
later bed is younger than the dike.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.400
The same rock thus constrains two different age relationships.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.180
Always ask whether you are observing an intrusion cutting a unit

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.040
or a fragment enclosed within it.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:26.220
Fossil ages and isotopic ages also bracket unconformities.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:30.960
The supplied textbook uses major boundaries at sixty-six, two hundred fifty-two

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.000
and five hundred forty-one million years.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.280
The older Connect label graphic has different numerical boundaries.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.840
Use that graphic for its original label positions, while keeping the

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.320
assigned textbook numbers separate and explicit.
